#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# vector_greater_equal

#% text
# `vector_greater_equal([a, b])` states that `a >= b` holds componentwise; a scalar side is compared with every component.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# With numbers it gives `true` or `false`; with symbols it stays as it is, a constraint for `n_minimize` and its relatives.

#% item
# A second argument `"NonNegativeCone"` is the componentwise order; in a constraint, `["NormCone", n]` states that the Euclidean norm of the first n - 1 components of a - b is at most its last.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Numbers:

#% cell
vector_greater_equal([[3, 2], [1, 2]])
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A constraint of a linear program:

#% cell
n_minimize([dot([1, 1], v), vector_greater_equal([dot([[1, 2], [1, 0]], v), [3, -1]])], v)
#% output
#= [1.0, [v -> [-1.0, 2.0]]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_minimize`, `vector_less_equal`, `greater_equal`
